## Voice files

A Supertonic voice is a style file — a set of learned tensors, not merely a
configuration. A cloned voice is derived from a recording of a real person,
and its `metadata` records the source recording it was extracted from.

Style files are gitignored (`.gitignore`: `supertonic-service/voice_styles/*.json`).
Install them per deployment, as described in `supertonic-service/README.md`.

Two obligations follow from the model license above and apply to generated
speech:

1. machine-generated audio must be disclosed as such;
2. a cloned voice must not be used to impersonate its speaker without that
   speaker's consent.

## User-provided Piper voice models

The Piper TTS service loads ONNX voice models from a directory the operator
supplies (`PIPER_MODELS_DIR`, defaulting to `./pipervoices`). Those models are
not tracked by this repository.

Each voice model has its own license, and the license is **not** the
`MODEL_CARD` in the model directory: those cards state the license of the
**dataset** a voice was trained on, which is not by itself a grant covering
the trained weights. Confirm the terms for each voice you deploy. The
upstream voice index at https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices is the
starting point.

Users are responsible for obtaining voice models legally and for their use
case being permitted.

## Runtime dependencies with copyleft or missing terms

These are not tracked or vendored by this repository, but they are pulled in
at install time and are relevant to anyone building a distribution.

### `piper-tts` — GPL-3.0-or-later

- `piper-service` imports `from piper.voice import PiperVoice`; the service
  does not work without it.
- It is **deliberately not declared** in `piper-service/requirements.txt`,
  which is why a fresh `pip install -r requirements.txt` yields a service that
  starts with zero voices and refuses every request. Install it yourself.
- The PyPI `piper-tts` distribution is the **GPL fork**
  (https://github.com/OHF-voice/piper1-gpl), licensed GPL-3.0-or-later. It
  bundles the `espeak-ng` engine data (also GPL-3.0) inside the wheel — measured
  in `piper_tts-1.7.0`: `piper/espeak-ng-data/` (125 language dictionaries) and
  a compiled `piper/espeakbridge.pyd`. Nothing needs installing separately, and
  no external `espeak-ng` is consulted.
- Consequence: the source in `piper-service/` remains MIT, but **a distributed
  artifact that combines it with `piper-tts` is a GPL-3.0-or-later combined
  work.** Distributing one means complying with the GPL for that artifact, and
  it cannot be represented as MIT-licensed as a whole.

### `soundfile` — bundles libsndfile (LGPL)

- `soundfile` itself is BSD-3-Clause, but its wheels ship a `libsndfile`
  binary under `_soundfile_data/`, alongside a verbatim copy of the
  **LGPL-2.1** (`_soundfile_data/COPYING`). The package metadata describes
  libsndfile as "a free, cross-platform, open-source (LGPL) library".
- The Windows build of soundfile 0.14.0 additionally carries codec code from
  `libmpg123` (LGPL-2.1), `libVorbis`, and `libFLAC` — measured as strings in
  `_soundfile_data/libsndfile_x64.dll`. Which codecs a build links varies by
  version and platform, so check the wheel you actually ship rather than
  relying on this list.
- Dynamically loading it is ordinarily workable for an MIT project, but the
  LGPL notice and source/relink offer are obligations of redistribution, and
  they are not currently discharged anywhere in this repository.

### `vig2p` — no license declared

- A transitive dependency of `kokoro-vietnamese`
  (`Requires-Dist: vig2p>=0.1.0`).
- Its distribution metadata declares no license, ships no `LICENSE` file, and
  records no author or project URL. Absent an explicit grant, default
  copyright applies and no redistribution right is granted.
- Worth resolving upstream before shipping a Kokoro-based distribution.

